WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:05.809
There have been very few consequences for
Donald Trump during his presidency for all

00:00:05.809 --> 00:00:07.379
of the horrible things he's done.

00:00:07.379 --> 00:00:12.459
And by this I mean the bad policies, the ignorant
and ridiculous statements, the embarrassing

00:00:12.459 --> 00:00:16.939
actions on the geopolitical stage, the lack
of decorum, the coarsening of the political

00:00:16.939 --> 00:00:18.380
discourse, all of it, right?

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.790
I mean all of it, all of it.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:22.359
There have been very few if any consequences.

00:00:22.359 --> 00:00:27.640
Now the most immediate and impactful consequences
there could be would be just voting Donald

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.220
Trump out in November of 2020 we've heard
from more and more farmers.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:37.870
I told you about this last week who are clearly
indirectly now saying we are getting crushed

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:39.160
and we know why.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.900
It's because of Donald Trump's tariffs.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:45.560
This is Donald Trump's fault that our businesses
are imploding and the markets in which we

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.400
operate are falling apart.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.640
That being said, I asked the question last
week, will those farmers actually hold Trump

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.360
accountable by voting for somebody else next
year?

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.080
You can be a lifelong Republican farmer that
is angry at Trump for his tariffs and for

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.269
destroying your business, but you might still
be unveiled, unwilling to vote for a Democrat

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:10.520
in November of 2020 we have to wait and see
what voters more broadly are thinking about

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.770
Donald Trump and the economy.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:16.929
May Beau bode well for the left in 2020 or
it may not.

00:01:16.929 --> 00:01:21.750
What I mean by that is there's a new poll
from Harvard caps Harris, which is sort of

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:22.750
positive.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:29.640
A majority of voters say, if we have a recession
in the next year, uh, we will blame Trump

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:35.560
more than anyone else and most people are
worried that indeed there will be a recession

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:36.560
in the next six months.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.250
Now, humans are notoriously bad at predicting
when the next recession is going to be.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.420
Economists included.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.069
We know every day we are one day closer to
the next one because that's how the system

00:01:46.069 --> 00:01:47.069
works.

00:01:47.069 --> 00:01:50.369
But by and large, we're not good at predicting
exactly when we are going to have a recession.

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:54.369
Sometimes we don't even realize we're in one
until we've been in it for a little bit.

00:01:54.369 --> 00:02:00.829
That being said, 57% of Americans would blame
Trump more than the federal reserve or anyone

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:01.829
else.

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:06.899
If the United States were to enter a recession
by the end of the year, 62% of Americans say

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:12.470
they are somewhat or very concerned that there
will be a recession in the next six months.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:18.670
Remember that Donald Trump has been insisting
that anything bad that happens in the economy

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:20.440
is the Federal Reserve's fault.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:25.240
Of course, the Federal Reserve is led by the
person that he selected to lead it.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:28.830
That's rarely mentioned when he talks about
that on tariffs.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:35.000
63% of Americans believe that the tariffs
are hurting the United States more than China.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:42.680
74% of Americans believe that it is American
consumers, not China that pays for the tariffs.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.250
Now, that's true.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:49.620
I'm actually shocked that 74% of Americans
understand this because Donald Trump has done

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:54.400
a good job to the extent that you need to
do a good job to convince ignorant Trumpists,

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.410
uh, of saying it's China who pays the tariffs,
even though of course, it is.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:03.210
Ultimately American consumers passed along
down from American companies and manufacturers

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.660
that are paying those tariffs.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.050
So all of this looks great, right?

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:08.800
People understand it's American consumers
paying the tariffs.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.730
People understand Trump is to blame people
understand if we have a recession, Trump would

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:15.030
be more responsible than anybody else.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:21.180
But then the question is, will the people
blaming Trump for the recession vote for a

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.520
Democrat in November of 2020?

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.460
And it is the same question that we have about
Republican farmers.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:36.000
Just because you accurately correctly precisely
blame Donald Trump for tariffs destroying

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.620
an economy doesn't mean all of a sudden that
a religious right Midwesterner is going to

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.710
go out and vote for a democrat.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:49.040
So the strategy I've outlined before, there
will be some disaffected Trumpists because

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.510
we have a country of 340 million people.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.540
Some disaffected Trump is are going to go
and vote for a democrat.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.540
Great.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:00.880
We'll take it some disaffected Trumpists are
going to stay home and not vote at all.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:05.610
That also helps us, assuming that we can get
out our base, but none of this counts as a

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:06.610
strategy.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:09.090
We must get our base out.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:10.090
That's it.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:13.820
As far as the economic stuff, we do actually
have some tools that are disposable to try

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.680
to mitigate how bad whenever the upcoming
recession it's coming.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:23.000
At some point we have tools that we can use
to mitigate the seriousness of the upcoming

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.000
recession.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.199
Uh, we can tax the wealthy through increasing
capital gains, through inheritance taxes,

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:32.020
through increasing top income brackets.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.229
We know that that's a tool that can be used.

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:35.740
Is there the political will to do it?

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.069
That's a different question.

00:04:37.069 --> 00:04:42.410
We can do infrastructure infrastructure spending
to soften a recession to create jobs to improve

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:43.539
communities.

00:04:43.539 --> 00:04:47.939
We can look at doing something with student
loan debt and medical debt to free up disposable

00:04:47.939 --> 00:04:52.410
income for people to try to push back against
the effects of a recession.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:58.250
We can deal with the cost of housing and the
rising costs of living to put more disposable

00:04:58.250 --> 00:04:59.400
income in people's pockets.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:03.229
So each of these is a big project that requires
a lot of discussion.

00:05:03.229 --> 00:05:10.830
But the point is we now have the 2008 recession
as another case study as in terms of helping

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:18.590
us know what tools that we have at our disposal
can lessen or soften the effect of a recession.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:21.340
Is Donald Trump willing to use any of those
tools?

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:26.719
It's not clear, but in the meantime we can
say it's good that people will correctly blame

00:05:26.719 --> 00:05:30.620
Trump most if we are to have a recession in
the next six to 12 months because that is

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:31.639
where the blame should be.

00:05:31.639 --> 00:05:34.999
We actually have specific economic policy
that we can tie to it.

00:05:34.999 --> 00:05:39.819
And then the broader question becomes, as
we are now 14 months out from the 2020 election,

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:46.240
will that blame translate not just into saying
you blame Trump, but into actually going to

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:50.949
the ballot box and saying, you know, on some
social issues, I don't agree with these Democrats

00:05:50.949 --> 00:05:53.349
or whoever it is that ultimately is the nominee.

00:05:53.349 --> 00:05:57.229
But for the good of the economy, I'm going
to vote for a democrat.

00:05:57.229 --> 00:05:58.360
That is the big question mark.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.039
And that's what I want to hear from you about.

00:06:00.039 --> 00:06:06.009
Will disaffected Trumpists to any significant
degree vote for Democrats, or are they more

00:06:06.009 --> 00:06:10.860
likely to just stay home or even vote for
Trump with the caveat that they don't like

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.469
everything he's been doing?

00:06:12.469 --> 00:06:13.469
Let me know.

00:06:13.469 --> 00:06:14.469
I'm on Twitter at d pacman.

00:06:14.469 --> 00:06:18.780
The show is on Twitter at David Pakman show
and make sure you're following us on twitch,

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:40.759
twitch.tv/david Pakman, four live streams,
uh, several times per week as well.

00:06:40.759 --> 00:07:32.809
[inaudible] hope to see you on one

00:07:32.809 --> 00:07:47.130
of them.

